  "query_preview": "..\nUpper level ridging will continue to promote a prolonged warming and \ndrying trend throughout the weekend and into next week. High \ntemperatures today will be in the mid to high 70s with some isolated \n80 degree spots in the deeper basin, around 10 degrees warmer than \nnormal for this time of year. Low temperatures will also remain \nnearly 10 degrees warmer then normal. Starting Sunday and continuing \nthrough Monday, the ridge will strengthen, increasing temperatures \nby another 4-6 degrees with highs in the high 70s to low 80s. On \nMonday, a weak disturbance will move through, flattening the ridge \nand resulting in afternoon wind gusts 10-15 mph throughout the \nregion with locally higher wind gusts near 20 mph. The flattening of \nthe ridge will also result in temperatures dropping a few degrees, \nbut will remain around 10 degrees higher than normal through \nThursday. On Friday, long range models show a cold front moving \nthrough, bringing a chance for gusty winds throughout the basin \nalongside a chance for rain showers. Should this front move through, \nmodels depict a nearly 10 degree decrease in temperatures from \nFriday to Saturday, much closer to normal for this time of year. \nPrecipitation chances remain low, generally 30 percent or less in \nthe mountains and even lower elsewhere. Overall, expect a beautiful, \nwarm fall week across the Pacific Northwest. /AS",
